Three tools come up most when people ask which AI coding assistant to pay for: GitHub Copilot, Cursor and Claude Code. Their list prices sit in a similar range, but they sell different things. Copilot is an assistant built around GitHub that works in editors, the command line and on github.com, Cursor is an editor built around AI, and Claude Code is a coding agent for the terminal and supported IDEs that comes bundled with Claude subscriptions. This guide compares the published plans and, more importantly, how each one limits usage. It uses list prices in US dollars from the vendors' own pages, checked on 11 November 2026. A disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, which makes Claude Code, one of the tools covered, and it was checked by a separate AI reviewer against the vendor pages. Stackbrook did not test these tools, and has no affiliate relationship with any of them at the time of writing.

## The prices at a glance

| Tool | Free tier | Entry paid plan | Top individual plan |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GitHub Copilot | Yes, limited | Pro, $10 a month | Max, $100 a month |
| Cursor | Yes, limited | Plus, $8 a month | Ultra, $200 a month |
| Claude (with Claude Code) | Chat only, no Claude Code | Pro, $20 a month | Ultra, $200 a month |

Prices change often and vary by region, tax and promotion, so treat the table as a snapshot. Team and business plans are priced separately and are not covered in detail here.

## GitHub Copilot

GitHub lists four individual paid plans. Copilot Pro is $10 a month, Pro+ is $39 and Max is $100. Copilot Free costs nothing and, per GitHub's page, includes 2,000 completions a month and access to lighter models. GitHub's licensing page lists Copilot Business for organizations at $19 per user per month, billed monthly.

The important change is how usage is measured. GitHub's documentation says paid plans come with a monthly allowance of GitHub AI Credits, where one credit equals one US cent. The docs list Pro with 1,000 base credits plus 500 flex credits, so 1,500 in total, and Pro+ with 3,900 base plus 3,100 flex, so 7,000 in total. That matches the $15 and $70 of total monthly credits GitHub's pricing page shows for the two plans, and the page shows $200 for Max. The flex part is variable usage on top of the base, and GitHub says flex allotments may change, so treat the totals as current figures, not guarantees. Copilot Pro is also free for some users, such as verified teachers and maintainers of popular open source projects, and verified students can get Copilot Student at no cost. Agent mode, code review, the cloud agent and the command-line tool draw down credits, and the amount used per request depends on the model. GitHub's pricing page says paid plans include unlimited code completions.

GitHub's documentation also keeps legacy pages describing an older request-based model, with a fixed number of premium requests per plan. GitHub's official community FAQ says all Copilot plans have moved to usage-based billing, so an older comparison that quotes premium-request allowances may be describing the earlier model, which can still apply to some existing subscribers. Check which model your account is on.

## Cursor

Cursor's help page describes two sets of plans. New customers see what it calls The X Subscription: Free, Plus at $8 a month ($80 a year), Premium at $30 ($300 a year), Super at $100 ($1,000 a year) and Ultra at $200 ($2,000 a year). The page describes included usage in multiples of Plus, with Premium at 4x, Super at 15x and Ultra at 50x. That is a relative figure, not a dollar amount, so it does not tell you how much work each plan covers.

Existing subscribers can stay on legacy plans: Pro at $20 a month, Pro+ at $60 and Ultra at $200. Cursor maps these to Premium, Super and Ultra on the new scheme. If you are an existing subscriber, you keep your plan until you switch or cancel, and Cursor says switching is permanent.

Two details matter when you compare. On the newer plans Cursor describes one weekly pool of usage for its own models, with third-party models paid at the model's API list price from prepaid credit on Premium and above. The Plus plan, per Cursor's page, runs Cursor's models at fixed settings and leaves out Auto, Cloud Agents, Automations, Bugbot, Origin and third-party models. On the legacy plans, usage comes from two monthly pools, one for Cursor's models and one for third-party models drawn down at API prices. So the same headline number, such as $20 a month, can mean different things depending on which scheme you are on.

## Claude Code

Claude Code is not sold on its own. It comes with a paid Claude subscription, and Anthropic's pricing page shows it as included on Pro, Max and Ultra and not on Free. Claude Code also works in supported IDEs, with usage counted against the same limits. Pro is $20 a month, or $17 a month when billed annually with $200 charged up front. Max is $100 a month with five times Pro's usage. Ultra is $200 a month with ten times Pro's weekly usage, and verified Ultra accounts have no five-hour limit.

Usage is shared. Anthropic's help article says the limits are shared across Claude and Claude Code, so a long coding session and your chats draw from the same pool. Pro and Max have a five-hour session limit and a weekly limit. When you reach a limit you can wait for the reset or turn on usage credits, which Anthropic describes as a way to keep working beyond the plan's included usage. Anthropic's help article also describes switching to a Claude Console account and buying API credits, billed at standard API rates. One catch from the help article: if an ANTHROPIC_API_KEY environment variable is set, Claude Code uses it instead of your subscription, which results in API charges. The same article says the monthly API credits included with the Max plan cannot be used in Claude Code or the Claude apps.

Because you are paying for Claude as a whole, the value depends on whether you also use the chat apps. If you only want a coding tool, the effective price is the full plan price.

## How the usage models differ

- **GitHub Copilot** counts spending in AI credits that vary with the model, and says code completions are unlimited on paid plans.
- **Cursor** uses relative multiples of its base plan on the new scheme and API-price pools on legacy plans, with third-party models paid separately on higher tiers.
- **Claude Code** shares a five-hour and weekly allowance with all your Claude use, and offers optional pay-as-you-go usage credits.

None of the three gives a fixed number of requests you can plan around. That makes list price a weak guide to what you will actually get. A plan that looks cheap can run out sooner if you lean on large models for long agent runs.

## Which should you pick

- If most of your work lives on GitHub and you want an assistant inside your existing editor, Copilot Pro at $10 (or Free) is the lowest-cost route to the paid features, and the Free plan is enough to try it.
- If you want an AI-first editor, Cursor Plus at $8 is the cheapest entry, but Plus excludes third-party models and several other features listed above, so a plan with third-party model access starts at Premium at $30 on the new scheme.
- If you already use Claude for writing or research and also want a terminal coding agent, Pro at $20 covers both in one subscription.
- If you hit limits regularly, compare the $100 tiers (Copilot Max, Cursor Super, Claude Max) before the $200 ones. They are the same price point across the three tools, though the usage they include is measured differently.

Before paying, start on a free tier or the cheapest plan for a month, watch the usage meter, and move up only if you hit the limits. Check the live pricing pages first, since all three vendors have changed their plans and billing rules this year.
